@noindent
Data-directed programming is the technique of designing programs to work with
such a table directly.  Previously, we implemented the mechanism that
interfaces the complex-arithmetic code with the two representation packages as
a set of procedures that each perform an explicit dispatch on type.  Here we
will implement the interface as a single procedure that looks up the
combination of the operation name and argument type in the table to find the
correct procedure to apply, and then applies it to the contents of the
argument.  If we do this, then to add a new representation package to the
system we need not change any existing procedures; we need only add new entries
to the table.

To implement this plan, this edition's runtime ships a real table, an
@code{OpTable}, keyed by the operation name plus the ordered list of type
tags (D19 of the edition plan), with two operations:

@itemize @bullet

@item
@code{table.put(op, tags, handler)} installs the handler in the table,
indexed by @code{op} and @code{tags}; a later @code{put} of the same pair
overwrites the earlier one.

@item
@code{table.get(op, tags)} looks up the @code{(op, tags)} entry in the table
and returns the handler found there.  If no entry is found, @code{get}
returns the absent option, @code{None}, never a false-ish sentinel.

@end itemize

@noindent
@code{OpTable} lives in the runtime shared by every chapter from here on; it
is not something Chapter 3's pairs-based tables (@ref{3.3.3}) build from
scratch, though the two share the same put/get discipline.

Here is how data-directed programming can be used in the complex-number system.
Ben, who developed the rectangular representation, implements his code just as
he did originally.  He defines a collection of procedures, or a
@newterm{package}, and interfaces these to the rest of the system by adding
entries to the table that tell the system how to operate on rectangular
numbers.  This is accomplished by calling the following procedure:

@example rust
fn selector_handler(f: fn(&Value) -> Result<Value, SchemeError>) -> Handler {
    Rc::new(move |args| f(sole(args)?))
}

fn constructor_handler(f: fn(f64, f64) -> Value) -> Handler {
    Rc::new(move |args| {
        let (x, y) = as_real_pair(args)?;
        Ok(f(x, y))
    })
}

pub fn install_rectangular_package(table: &OpTable) {
    let tag = tag_list_key(&["rectangular"]);
    table.put(Key::sym("real-part"), tag.clone(), selector_handler(rect_real_part));
    table.put(Key::sym("imag-part"), tag.clone(), selector_handler(rect_imag_part));
    table.put(Key::sym("magnitude"), tag.clone(), selector_handler(rect_magnitude));
    table.put(Key::sym("angle"), tag, selector_handler(rect_angle));
    table.put(
        Key::sym("make-from-real-imag"),
        Key::sym("rectangular"),
        constructor_handler(rect_make_from_real_imag_tagged),
    );
    table.put(
        Key::sym("make-from-mag-ang"),
        Key::sym("rectangular"),
        constructor_handler(rect_make_from_mag_ang_tagged),
    );
}
@end example

@noindent
Notice that the internal procedures here are the same procedures from
@ref{2.4.1} that Ben wrote when he was working in isolation.  No changes are
necessary to interface them to the rest of the system. Moreover,
since @code{rect_real_part} and friends are private free functions, Ben
needn't worry about name conflicts with other procedures outside this module.
To interface these to the rest of the system, Ben
installs his @code{real-part} procedure under the operation name
@code{real-part} and the tag list @code{(rectangular)}, and similarly for the other
selectors.@footnote{We install under the one-element tag list
@code{(rectangular)} rather than the bare symbol @code{rectangular} to allow
for the possibility of operations with multiple arguments, not all of the
same type.}  The interface also defines the
constructors to be used by the external system.@footnote{The tag the
constructors are installed under needn't be a list because a constructor is
always used to make an object of one particular type.}  These are identical to
Ben's internally defined constructors, except that they attach the tag.

Alyssa's polar package is analogous:

@example rust
pub fn install_polar_package(table: &OpTable) {
    let tag = tag_list_key(&["polar"]);
    table.put(Key::sym("real-part"), tag.clone(), selector_handler(polar_real_part));
    table.put(Key::sym("imag-part"), tag.clone(), selector_handler(polar_imag_part));
    table.put(Key::sym("magnitude"), tag.clone(), selector_handler(polar_magnitude));
    table.put(Key::sym("angle"), tag, selector_handler(polar_angle));
    table.put(
        Key::sym("make-from-real-imag"),
        Key::sym("polar"),
        constructor_handler(polar_make_from_real_imag_tagged),
    );
    table.put(
        Key::sym("make-from-mag-ang"),
        Key::sym("polar"),
        constructor_handler(polar_make_from_mag_ang_tagged),
    );
}
@end example

@noindent
Even though Ben and Alyssa both still use their original procedures defined
with the same names as each other's (e.g., @code{real_part}), these definitions
are private to different modules, so there is no name conflict.

The complex-arithmetic selectors access the table by means of a general
``operation'' procedure called @code{apply_generic}, which applies a generic
operation to some arguments.  @code{apply_generic} looks in the table under the
name of the operation and the tags of the arguments and applies the resulting
handler if one is present:@footnote{The book's @code{apply-generic} uses the
dotted-tail notation of @ref{Exercise 2.20} because different generic
operations take different numbers of arguments; this edition's
@code{apply_generic} takes the operation name and a slice of arguments
directly, since a slice already stands for a list of arguments of any
length, and the book's separate @code{apply} primitive becomes the ordinary
call @code{handler(&bare)} against the contents slice.}

@example rust
pub fn apply_generic(table: &OpTable, op: &str, args: &[Value]) -> Result<Value, SchemeError> {
    let mut tag_keys = Vec::with_capacity(args.len());
    let mut tag_values = Vec::with_capacity(args.len());
    for arg in args {
        let tag = type_tag(arg)?;
        tag_values.push(Value::Sym(tag.clone()));
        tag_keys.push(Key::Sym(tag));
    }
    let Some(handler) = table.get(&Key::sym(op), &key_tag_list(&tag_keys)) else {
        return Err(SchemeError::UserRaised {
            message: "No method for these types: APPLY-GENERIC".into(),
            irritants: vec![Value::list(vec![Value::sym(op), Value::list(tag_values)])],
        });
    };
    let bare: Vec<Value> = args.iter().map(contents).collect::<Result<_, _>>()?;
    handler(&bare)
}
@end example

@noindent
A miss here does not fall through to a general-purpose error: it raises the
edition's typed @code{SchemeError::UserRaised}, naming the operation and the
tag list that had no handler, which is the concrete answer to what an
extensible-at-runtime table costs that a closed trait cannot: the compiler
cannot catch a missing combination the way it catches a missing @code{match}
arm, so the table's own miss path has to.

Using @code{apply_generic}, we can define our generic selectors as follows:

@example rust
pub fn real_part(table: &OpTable, z: &Value) -> Result<Value, SchemeError> {
    apply_generic(table, "real-part", std::slice::from_ref(z))
}
pub fn imag_part(table: &OpTable, z: &Value) -> Result<Value, SchemeError> {
    apply_generic(table, "imag-part", std::slice::from_ref(z))
}
pub fn magnitude(table: &OpTable, z: &Value) -> Result<Value, SchemeError> {
    apply_generic(table, "magnitude", std::slice::from_ref(z))
}
pub fn angle(table: &OpTable, z: &Value) -> Result<Value, SchemeError> {
    apply_generic(table, "angle", std::slice::from_ref(z))
}
@end example

@noindent
Observe that these do not change at all if a new representation is added to the
system.

We can also extract from the table the constructors to be used by the programs
external to the packages in making complex numbers from real and imaginary
parts and from magnitudes and angles.  As in @ref{2.4.2}, we construct
rectangular numbers whenever we have real and imaginary parts, and polar
numbers whenever we have magnitudes and angles:

@example rust
pub fn make_from_real_imag(table: &OpTable, x: f64, y: f64) -> Result<Value, SchemeError> {
    let Some(handler) = table.get(&Key::sym("make-from-real-imag"), &Key::sym("rectangular"))
    else {
        return Err(SchemeError::UserRaised {
            message: "make-from-real-imag is not installed for rectangular".into(),
            irritants: vec![],
        });
    };
    handler(&[Value::real(x), Value::real(y)])
}

pub fn make_from_mag_ang(table: &OpTable, r: f64, a: f64) -> Result<Value, SchemeError> {
    let Some(handler) = table.get(&Key::sym("make-from-mag-ang"), &Key::sym("polar")) else {
        return Err(SchemeError::UserRaised {
            message: "make-from-mag-ang is not installed for polar".into(),
            irritants: vec![],
        });
    };
    handler(&[Value::real(r), Value::real(a)])
}
@end example

@quotation
@strong{In this language: runtime dispatch through the operation table.}

Rust's traits close the set of participating types once the crate compiles,
so the section's open, grow-at-runtime dispatch has to enter as data: the
runtime's @code{OpTable} maps @code{(operation, tag-list)} keys to
@code{Rc<dyn Fn>} handlers that return the edition's typed error, exactly
like the book's own table.

@example rust
table.put(Key::sym("real-part"), tag_list_key(&["rectangular"]), handler);
// => ()
table.get(&Key::sym("real-part"), &tag_list_key(&["polar"]));
// => None
@end example

The book's table still wins whenever packages are installed by code the
compiler never saw together: plugins, or a second edition's crate loaded at
run time.  When the type set is closed and known in advance, a plain
@code{enum} with an exhaustive @code{match} is both faster and total, with
the compiler proving every case handled; exercise 2.76 measures exactly that
trade.
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 2.73}Exercise 2.73:} @ref{2.3.2} described a
program that performs symbolic differentiation over the enum @code{Expr}:

@example rust
pub fn deriv(exp: &Expr, var: &Symbol) -> Result<Expr, SchemeError> {
    match exp {
        Expr::Num(_) => Ok(Expr::Num(0)),
        Expr::Var(x) => Ok(Expr::Num(i128::from(x == var))),
        Expr::Sum(a1, a2) => make_sum(deriv(a1, var)?, deriv(a2, var)?),
        Expr::Product(m1, m2) => {
            let left = make_product((**m1).clone(), deriv(m2, var)?)?;
            let right = make_product(deriv(m1, var)?, (**m2).clone())?;
            make_sum(left, right)
        }
    }
}
@end example

We can regard this program as performing a dispatch on the type of the
expression to be differentiated.  In this situation the ``type tag'' of the
datum is the algebraic operator symbol (such as @code{+}) and the operation
being performed is @code{deriv}.  We can transform this program into
data-directed style by rewriting the basic derivative procedure as

@example rust
pub fn deriv(exp: &Expr, var: &Symbol, table: &DerivTable) -> Result<Expr, SchemeError> {
    match exp {
        Expr::Num(_) => Ok(Expr::Num(0)),
        Expr::Var(x) => Ok(Expr::Num(i128::from(x == var))),
        _ => {
            let Some(op) = operator(exp) else {
                return Err(unknown_expression(exp));
            };
            let Some(handler) = deriv_get(table, "deriv", op) else {
                return Err(unknown_expression(exp));
            };
            handler(table, &operands(exp), var)
        }
    }
}

pub fn operator(exp: &Expr) -> Option<&'static str> {
    match exp {
        Expr::Sum(..) => Some("+"),
        Expr::Product(..) => Some("*"),
        Expr::Num(_) | Expr::Var(_) => None,
    }
}
pub fn operands(exp: &Expr) -> Vec<&Expr> {
    match exp {
        Expr::Sum(a, b) | Expr::Product(a, b) => vec![a, b],
        Expr::Num(_) | Expr::Var(_) => vec![],
    }
}
@end example

@enumerate a

@item
Explain what was done above.  Why can't we assimilate the cases
@code{Expr::Num} and @code{Expr::Var} into the data-directed dispatch?

@item
Write the handlers for derivatives of sums and products, and the auxiliary
code required to install them in the table used by the program above.

@item
Choose any additional differentiation rule that you like, such as the one for
exponents (@ref{Exercise 2.56}), and install it in this data-directed
system.

@item
In this simple algebraic manipulator the type of an expression is the algebraic
operator that binds it together.  Suppose, however, we indexed the handlers
in the opposite way, so that the dispatch line in @code{deriv} looked like

@example rust
let Some(handler) = deriv_get(table, op, "deriv") else { ... };
@end example

@noindent
What corresponding changes to the derivative system are required?

@end enumerate
@end quotation

